.TH ASK 1
.SH NAME
ask, llmapi \- put a question to a language model
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ask
[
.B -ns
] [
.B -m
.I model
] [
.I system
]
.br
.B llmapi
[
.B -ns
] [
.B -b
.BR anthropic | openai
] [
.B -u
.I url
] [
.B -m
.I model
] [
.B -t
.I maxtokens
] [
.I system
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ask
puts the text on its standard input to a language model, with the
instructions in the file
.IR system ,
and prints the answer.
A tool that wants a model's answer pipes to it, and so needs no
particular service and does not know which model it has.
.B $llm
says which way, as one line of text:
.TP
.B llm
the model mounted at
.B /mnt/llm
.RI ( llmsrv ,
or one mounted from another machine): a session is cloned from
.BR new ,
the instructions written to its
.BR system ,
the question to its
.BR ask ,
the answer read back, and the session closed;
.TP
.BR anthropic " [\fIurl\fP]"
the Anthropic API, called from here by
.IR llmapi ;
.TP
.BI openai " url"
an OpenAI-compatible API (Ollama, vLLM), the same way;
.TP
anything else
a command, given the instructions, a blank line and the question on its
standard input; what it prints is the answer.
Where the command has a word
.BR {system} ,
the instructions are put there as one argument and only the question
goes on its input: the host's Claude command is a plain model as
\f5os claude -p --tools '' --system-prompt {system}\fP.
.PP
Unset, it is
.B llm
when
.B /mnt/llm/new
is there, and
.B anthropic
otherwise.
.B -m
names the model.
.B -n
prints only the model's name.
.B -s
prints only the settings the question is asked with, one line of
.IB attr = value
words, the same three whatever the way:
.EX
	temperature=default maxtokens=8192 thinking=off
.EE
read from the session's files for
.BR llm ,
and each
.B unknown
for a command, whose settings are its own.
A tool that keeps a record of what a model wrote puts the model's name
and this line beside it.
.PP
.I Llmapi
calls a model's API directly, with no service: the Anthropic Messages
API
.RB ( -b
.BR anthropic ,
the default;
.B -u
for another address), or an OpenAI-compatible one
.RB ( -b
.B openai
.B -u
.IR url ).
The key is factotum's
.RB ( "proto=pass service=anthropic" ,
or
.BR service=openai ),
or failing that
.B $ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
or
.BR $OPENAI_API_KEY ;
a local OpenAI-compatible server needs none.
No temperature is sent: the model's own is used.
.B -t
caps the answer's length (8192 tokens).
.B -n
and
.B -s
are as for
.IR ask .
.SH EXAMPLES
A command's answer, with no model at all, as a test gives one:
.EX
	llm='cat > /dev/null; echo yes'
	echo 'Is it raining?' | ask
.EE
A local model:
.EX
	llm='openai http://localhost:11434/v1'
	echo 'Name a prime.' | ask -m llama3.2 /lib/myprompt
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /dis/ask
.br
.B /appl/cmd/llmapi.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR factotum (4);
.BR llmsrv 's
files, in
.B docs/llm-mount.md
in the source tree
.SH BUGS
A command run by
.I ask
is the host's, if it begins
.BR os ,
and not confined by the caller's name space.
